ncy, which means no cap yet.\n\nActually, a latency of ~10ms is very common for SATA SSDs. A SATA SSD at QD1 has ~0.5ms latency and 10k IOPS. At QD32, it reaches ~100k IOPS with ~0.3ms latency. So 10ms is way too high for SATA SSD.\n\nUnless... the filesystem is doing something? If fio is using buffered IO without direct=1, then 4k random read might involve page cache misses and readahead? No, random read usually bypasses cache if direct=1. If not direct=1, then it could be weird.\n\nBut the bandwidth numbers being off by 1024 suggests maybe the whole dataset is from a simulator or has a scaling bug.\n\nRegardless, I must analyze the data as given but point out the physical anomaly.\n\nActually, looking at the data again: bw_mib for seqread is 4.09. If we assume the unit is actually MiB/s, then 4.09 MiB/s with 128k block means IOPS = 4.09*1024/128 = 32.7 IOPS. But reported IOPS is 33,507. So the IOPS and BW are inconsistent by exactly a factor of 1024. This strongly suggests the bw values were divided by 1024 one extra time. So 4.09 should be 4.09 GiB/s. I will mention this as \"原始记录疑似单位为 GiB/s\" or just convert it properly.\n\nWait, for randread: bw_mib 0.2. If multiplied by 1024 = 204.8 MiB/s. 51,616 * 4k = 201.6 MiB/s. Close enough (rounding).\nFor randwrite: 0.13 * 1024 = 133.12 MiB/s. 35,383 * 4k = 138.2 MiB/s. Slight difference but close.\nFor seqwrite: 0.92 * 1024 = 942 MiB/s. 7,527 * 128k = 940.9 MiB/s. Very close.\n\nSo yes, the bw_mib values are actually in GiB/s for large values, or more likely, the fio JSON output was misinterpreted. In fio's JSON output, bandwidth is sometimes in bytes per second or kilobytes. If the extraction script divided by 1024 twice, you get this. Anyway, I'll present the corrected bandwidth.\n\nLet me craft the response now.",
